# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Security headers — apply to all routes
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Ported from the Blazor PWA's own `_headers` (T-53 stage 4: Flutter Web takes
# over the hostname, so it also takes over the promises the hostname made).
# The CSP is NOT a copy — the engine is different:
#   * `wasm-unsafe-eval` + `blob:` in script-src/worker-src: CanvasKit compiles
#     WebAssembly and Flutter spawns workers from blob URLs.
#   * `'unsafe-eval'` is NOT here — Blazor WASM needed it, CanvasKit does not.
#   * canvaskit is served from THIS origin (the deploy builds with
#     `--no-web-resources-cdn`) — no third-party SCRIPT source, ever.
#   * `fonts.gstatic.com` in connect-src/font-src is NOT decoration: CanvasKit
#     draws text itself, so a glyph the embedded Inter does not cover (the
#     subset is `latin`, U-27) makes the engine FETCH a fallback font — Roboto
#     and Noto Sans Symbols. Blocked, the product's own emoji vocabulary
#     (🔔 ⏳ 🛡️ 🗑️ 📄) renders as tofu on the web channel; Android never sees
#     this because the glyph comes from the system font. Owner decision
#     (23/08/2026): allow the host — which makes Google an operator for the web
#     client, declared in the privacy policy. It is fetched with `fetch`, so
#     `connect-src` is the directive that actually rules; `font-src` is there
#     for the CSS path.
#   * T-62 adds TWO connect-src hosts and NOTHING to script-src, which is the
#     whole shape of that item: `firebase_core_web` fetches the Firebase JS SDK
#     from gstatic by dynamic `import()`, and this CSP forbids executable code
#     from anywhere but this origin — so the SDK is VENDORED under
#     `/firebasejs/<version>/` instead (`tool/vendor_firebase_js.py`) and the
#     plugin is told to inject nothing. What remains third-party is the API the
#     SDK TALKS to: `firebaseinstallations` mints the installation id and
#     `fcmregistrations` exchanges it for the registration token. Both are
#     `fetch`, from the page AND from the messaging worker (a worker's requests
#     are judged by the CSP served with the worker script, which is this one).
#   * T-66 adds ONE connect-src host and nothing else: the Sentry ingest
#     endpoint the crash reporter POSTs its envelope to. There is no SDK and no
#     third-party SCRIPT — the payload is built by our own code — so this is the
#     same shape as the T-62 line above: what is third-party is the API we TALK
#     to. Without this source the browser blocks the POST and the web channel
#     reports NOTHING, silently, because a reporter that may never break the app
#     swallows its own transport failure. `web_channel_test` reads the host out
#     of `Env.sentryDsn` and fails the build if the two ever drift.
#   * F-71 (Fulcrum 02.3) — the ONE third-party script this CSP admits, and
#     only its exact path: `https://accounts.google.com/gsi/client`. The native
#     Google door on the web hands its ID token only to Google's own button
#     (GIS `renderButton`), which the owner accepted on 24/09/2026, and Google
#     does not allow the GIS client to be self-hosted — so the T-62 answer
#     (vendor it) does not exist here. Around it, Google's published CSP for
#     GIS and nothing wider: the button's iframe (`frame-src .../gsi/`), its
#     stylesheet (`style-src .../gsi/style`) and its calls (`connect-src
#     .../gsi/`). `frame-src` restates `'self' blob:` because naming it
#     REPLACES the `child-src` fallback for frames (the PDF print iframe is a
#     blob). The script only loads on a page that shows the button — the app
#     initializes the plugin lazily. `web_channel_test` pins each path.
#   * Fulcrum 03.4 — the API is the Fulcrum GATEWAY, not Supabase. Both
#     flavours name their gateway host (`api.entrelares.app` for the build
#     `deploy-web` publishes, `api-dev.entrelares.app` for the QA build that
#     ships this same file), each over https and wss (REST, Auth, Functions,
#     Realtime all ride the same host). `*.supabase.co` LEFT this list on
#     purpose: no request of the app goes there any more, and a CSP that still
#     admitted it would let a stray project URL work in the browser while the
#     gateway was meant to be the only door. `web_channel_test` pins both
#     halves — each flavour's host covered, and no supabase.co at all.
#   * L-29 — `X-Robots-Tag: noindex`, on EVERY path. `_redirects` answers 200
#     with the same `index.html` for any address, so to a crawler this host is
#     an endless set of identical pages titled "Entrelares" with no text (the
#     app draws on a canvas) — and it was already in Bing's index beside the
#     landing, competing for the brand name. The landing (`entrelares.app`) is
#     the one search result this product wants. A HEADER rather than a meta
#     tag: it covers the rewritten routes, the JSON and the workers alike, and
#     it lives in the one block this file already applies to every path. It
#     does not touch the App Link check — Android reads `assetlinks.json`, not
#     the index. Its pair is `robots.txt`, which must keep ALLOWING the crawl: a
#     crawler that may not fetch a page never sees the header that drops it.
/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' blob: https://accounts.google.com/gsi/client; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://accounts.google.com/gsi/style; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self' data: https://fonts.gstatic.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; child-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self' blob: https://accounts.google.com/gsi/; connect-src 'self' https://api.entrelares.app wss://api.entrelares.app https://api-dev.entrelares.app wss://api-dev.entrelares.app https://cloud.umami.is https://fonts.gstatic.com https://firebaseinstallations.googleapis.com https://fcmregistrations.googleapis.com https://o4511910022217728.ingest.us.sentry.io https://accounts.google.com/gsi/; frame-ancestors 'none'

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Cache control
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The TOMBSTONE of the Blazor service worker, and the reason the cutover
# reaches installed PWAs at all: the browser re-fetches this URL on its update
# check, and it must never come from a cache. See service-worker.js.
/service-worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

# Flutter's own service worker and the bootstrap that carries its version. A
# stale copy of either pins the app shell to an old build.
/flutter_service_worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

/flutter_bootstrap.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

# T-62 — the FCM worker. Same rule as the two above and for the same reason:
# the browser re-fetches a registered worker to check it for updates, and a
# cached copy pins the push routing (and the Firebase config) to an old build.
/firebase-messaging-sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

# The app shell must be fresh so the service worker can always serve the
# correct version.
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

# T-62 — the vendored Firebase JS SDK. The exact opposite rule of the four
# above, and safely so: the SDK VERSION is in the path, so a given URL's bytes
# never change and a new version is a new URL. Cached hard, it costs a returning
# visitor nothing; the files are only ever fetched by someone who has push on.
/firebasejs/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Digital Asset Links — Android re-reads it to verify App Links. Served as
# JSON, never as a download, and never stale for longer than a day.
/.well-known/assetlinks.json
  Content-Type: application/json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
